From gregory_r_warnes at groton.pfizer.com  Mon Oct  8 15:28:43 2001
From: gregory_r_warnes at groton.pfizer.com (Warnes, Gregory R)
Date: Mon, 08 Oct 2001 11:28:43 -0400
Subject: Re:
Message-ID: <5429125E11E4D411AF7300805FE603A8014CEEB4@groexmbcr02.pfizer.com>

There are three basic methods of handling a varying number of arguments.
Which is best depends on what you need to do.

1) give arguments default values:

        f <- function(x, y=NULL, z=NULL)
        {
           return( c(x,y,z) )
        }

2) check if arguments were given using missing():

        f <- function(x, y, z)
        {
           if( missing(y) && missing(z) )
                return( c(x) )
           else if( missing(z) )
                        return( c(x,y) )
               else
                return( c(x,y,z) )
        }

3) use "...", which means 'all other arguments' 

        f <- function(x, ...)
        {
           return( c(x,...) )
            }

