Switzerland

Tel.   +41-61-68.80396
Fax   +41-61-68.82321
email ansgar.hebborn at roche.com

=======================================================================================================
My request was as follows: 

___________________________________________________________________________________
*       I have a vector with time points called T<-1:10.
*       I have another vector with 5 observations called F<-(1,2,6,7,10) 
representing the failure times for each of the 5 subjects
*       Now I would like to know how many subjects survive beyond each point in 
time. This resulting vector S ("survival") should have 10 observations, 1 for 
each timepoint, and would look as follows
        survival S-> (4, 3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 1, 1, 1, 0)

How do I get there without using a loop? 

With a loop it could look like:

S1<-NULL
T1<-1:10
F1<-c(1,2,6,7,10)
for(i in T1) {S1[i]<-length(F1[F1>i])}
S1
rm(S1,T1,F1)

Any suggestions for a vectorized solution?
__________________________________________________________________________________________

THE ANSWERS:

________________________________________________
Terry Therneau suggested:
-------------------------
 Why not use the survival functions?
 
 data   <- c(1,2,6,7,10)
 wanted <- 1:10
 
 fit <- survfit(Surv(data) ~1)
 summary(fit, times=wanted, extend=T)$n.risk

 [1] 4 3 3 3 3 2 1 1 1 0

 This will work for very large vectors.
 

________________________________________________
John Fox replied:
------------------------
I assume that T1 need not contain successive integers. How about:

     > sapply(T1, function(i) sum(F1 > i))
     [1] 4 3 3 3 3 2 1 1 1 0
     >

________________________________________________
Erin Hodgess suggested:
------------------------
> apply(apply(F2,1,FUN=function(x){x >T1}),1,sum)

 [1] 4 3 3 3 3 2 1 1 1 0

________________________________________________
Bert Gunter mentioned the close relationship between the "apply" functions and 
the "for" loops:
------------------------
I can think of a "clever" way using outer() too -- something like :
apply(outer(T,F,'<='),1,sum) 
(maybe the <= need to be '>' -- I forget which way outer works)

-- but the question is, why bother? Both my version and John Fox's involve
an application of (l)apply, which is still a hidden for() loop, not a
vectorized calculation. True vectorization shifts the looping to the
underlying C code, rather than in S. As Chambers's book, V&R's S
Programming, and other references make clear, there is very little if any
gain in efficiency from using apply commands vs explicit looping except in
very large loops (thousands of iterations), where memory buildup may become
a problem. The chief advantage to apply() is clarity of code and
programming, and in this case, your for() loop is quite satisfactory.

________________________________________________
Bill Dunlop's response was:
------------------------
The which.just.above function gets you pretty close.  It does some
sorting to avoid loops.

which.just.above <- function(x, reference, strict = T) {
# output[k] will be index of smallest value in reference vector
# larger than x[k].  If strict=F, replace 'larger than' by
# 'larger than or equal to'.
# We should allow NA's in x (but we don't). NA's in reference
# should not be allowed.
        if(any(is.na(x)) || any(is.na(reference))) stop("NA's in input")
        if(strict)
                i <- c(rep(T, length(reference)), rep(F, length(x)))[order(c(
                        reference, x))]
        else i <- c(rep(F, length(x)), rep(T, length(reference)))[order(c(x,
                        reference))]
        i <- cumsum(i)[!i] + 1
        i[i > length(reference)] <- NA
        # i is length of x and has values in range 1:length(reference) or NA
# following needed if reference is not sorted
        i <- order(reference)[i]
# following needed if x is not sorted
        i[order(order(x))]
}

E.g., your algorithm is equivalent to the function f1:

f1 <- function(Time, Failure)
{
        S1 <- integer(length(Time))
        for(i in Time) {
                S1[i] <- length(Failure[Failure > i])
        }
        S1
}

and you can define f2 to use which.just.above:

f2 <-
function(Time, Failure)
{
        1 + length(Failure) - which.just.above(Time, Failure, strict = T)
}

For your data they give the same result except that f2() gives NA where yours
gives 0 and that could easily be patched up.

