From Nick.Ellis at csiro.au  Tue Oct 23 00:48:32 2001
From: Nick.Ellis at csiro.au
Date: Tue, 23 Oct 2001 10:48:32 +1000
Subject: Re: Grep and inverted commas
Message-ID: <745A64FABDC57D4E83A71D1B613687B25861B3@apogon.bne.marine.csiro.au>

First, you can get funny characters into strings by escaping them (2,3,4)
below

> str <- scan(what="")
1: //,/
2: \\
3: \'
4: \"
5: asdajsd
6: ajdlads
7: 
> str
[1] "//,/"    "\\"      "'"       "\""      "asdajsd" "ajdlads"

str[2] is really a single backslash

> nchar(str)
[1] 4 1 1 1 7 7

Now to use grep. Forward slashes, commas and ordinary characters are not
special. Even a single quote inside double quotes is OK.

> grep("a",str)
[1] 5 6
> grep("/",str)
[1] 1
> grep("'",str)
[1] 3

To get the double quote, either wrap in single quotes or escape it with a
backslash

> grep('"',str)
[1] 4
> grep("\"",str)
[1] 4

For the backslash, either use [....] notation (match any character inside
the brackets) remembering to escape the backslash...

> grep("[\\]",str)
[1] 2

... or construct a grep expression \\ that matches a backslash. Of course,
you have to escape each one!

> grep("\\\\",str)
[1] 2

This one won't work because it becomes a single \ which isn't a proper
regular expression.

> grep("\\",str)
Problem in .C("S_grep",: Cannot compile pattern: Regular expression ends
prematurely, while calling subroutine S_grep

