From myotis at cix.co.uk  Thu Oct 25 11:56:00 2001
From: myotis at cix.co.uk (Graham M Smith)
Date: Thu, 25 Oct 2001 12:56:00 +0100
Subject: Summary: S-plus and Arcview
Message-ID: <memo.20011025125605.16681D@myotis.compulink.co.uk>

I asked about using S-plus and arcview together.

S-plus provide a specialist Arcview extension, but this is rather 
expensive,  I have also just discovered it is no longer available for 
S-Plus 2000, so if you want to go that route you will need to upgrade to 
S-Plus 6.

They were few respondents and most simply recommended using the Arcview 
extension.

Tom Stockton gave more details and his response is below:
----------------------------------------------------------

S in much better at spatial statistics and plotting than ArcView so I like
doing the spatial plotting and statistics in S using imported Arc 
coverages
and shapes files. Usually I manipulate the mapping info in ArcView and
convert polygons to points which can be read into S (shape files have 
DBase
attribute tables) and plotted (e.g., lines()). There are some nuances but
this works well. Lately I've been using R (Open Source & free) and the
RArcInfo package (Open Source & free) which reads ArcInfo coverages (but 
not
shape files) directly. I've semi looked into reading shape files directly
into S but haven't made much progress though it should be feasible using
existing shape file libraries.

Couple other pointers:
Attached is an ArcView extenstion that simplies converting polygons to
points. (goes in D:\ESRI\AV_GIS30\ARCVIEW\EXT32)
Once you have converted to a points shape file you need to add the x and y
coordinates. There is an Avenue script in something like 
D:\ESRI\AV_GIS30\ARCVIEW\Samples\scripts\addxycoo.ave that will add the
x and y coordinates to the point attribute table.
-----------------------------------------------------
I haven't attached the extension mentioned, but it is called Poly2pts.avx

Cheers,

Graham S

