From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Sun Oct 28 13:58:01 2001
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Sun, 28 Oct 2001 13:58:01 +0000
Subject: Re: Error in k-means clustering
In-Reply-To: <3BDB7545.231D5BCC@gmu.edu>
References: <3BDB7545.231D5BCC@gmu.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.WNT.4.31.0110281348170.1108-100000@tern.stats>

On Sat, 27 Oct 2001, James A Shine wrote:

> Hello,
>   Splus 2000, Release 2, on Windows 2000 Professional.
>   A group of us at George Mason University have been using S-Plus to
> classify data sets by trees and clustering. We have attempted to perform
>
> a k-means cluster on a large (18298 x 10) data set and get an error
> message, "requested dynamic memory 659MB unavailable". We have tried
> changing the virtual memory settings on the machine but this was
> unsuccessful. The machine is a WinBook laptop with 128MB of RAM and a 20
> GB
> hard drive with 5 GB free. Is there anything we can do to get around
> this problem, or  do we need more RAM to run a k-means on this size data
>
> set?

You need to specify the initial centres.  Otherwise it will do a
hierachical clustering, and that will need the distance matrix
(at over 1.3Gb, you are likely hitting the 2Gb limit for 32-bit versions of
S-PLUS).

A quick test showed that this will run, but use increasing amounts of
memory.  I doubt if it will complete in the resources you have.  I would
certainly start by clustering a small random subset and use the result as
initial cluster centres for the big problem.  You might never get up to
18298 rows, but it is quite possible that a 10% sample would suffice.

-- 
