Subject: Re: Error in k-means clustering
Message-ID: <5.1.0.14.2.20011029141204.01e97008@Pop.mcit.com>

Here's a related topic (well, the relation is tenuous, but my thresholds
are quite low)

TFL!  GTST!  BF tomorrow!

_______________________

On Sat, 27 Oct 2001, James A Shine wrote:

> Hello,

>   Splus 2000, Release 2, on Windows 2000
Professional.

>   A group of us at George Mason University have been using
S-Plus to

> classify data sets by trees and clustering. We have attempted to
perform

>

> a k-means cluster on a large (18298 x 10) data set and get an
error

> message, "requested dynamic memory 659MB unavailable". We
have tried

> changing the virtual memory settings on the machine but this
was

> unsuccessful. The machine is a WinBook laptop with 128MB of RAM and
a 20

> GB

> hard drive with 5 GB free. Is there anything we can do to get
around

> this problem, or  do we need more RAM to run a k-means on this
size data

>

> set?

You need to specify the initial centres.  Otherwise it will do
a

hierachical clustering, and that will need the distance matrix

(at over 1.3Gb, you are likely hitting the 2Gb limit for 32-bit
versions of

S-PLUS).

A quick test showed that this will run, but use increasing amounts
of

memory.  I doubt if it will complete in the resources you
have.  I would

certainly start by clustering a small random subset and use the result
as

initial cluster centres for the big problem.  You might never get up
to

18298 rows, but it is quite possible that a 10% sample would
suffice.

-- 

