From jones at reed.edu  Tue Oct 30 18:29:30 2001
From: jones at reed.edu (Albyn Jones)
Date: Tue, 30 Oct 2001 10:29:30 -0800
Subject: independent variables
Message-ID: <20011030102930.R441@sellwood.reed.edu>

unfortunately I deleted the original message about polynomials
in glm's, so this is possibly missing the point of the question...

it appeared to me that the question derived from the confusion
caused by the two unrelated meanings of  "independent variable" 
  1) in a regression or glm: "explanatory variable"
  2) in probability: independent random variables satisfy 
                     Pr(X < a, Y < b) = Pr(X < a)Pr( Y < b)
                     for all a and b.

a polynomial fit like Y ~ X + X^2 + X^3
does not violate any model assumptions, because the "independent
variables" (ie explanatory variables) are not assumed to be
"independent random variables" in the sense of probability theory.
