From Bill.Venables at CMIS.CSIRO.AU  Wed Oct 31 05:42:29 2001
From: Bill.Venables at CMIS.CSIRO.AU
Date: Wed, 31 Oct 2001 15:42:29 +1000
Subject: Re: Quadratic terms in GLM
Message-ID: <E09E527B56BE2D438A3D6A246DDD27A90491A8@Roper-CV.qld.cmis.csiro.au>

Wilfried,

You have been misled by the laziness of the English language.  

"Independent" is a much over-used word and means many different things in
different contexts.  Here it simply means "not the dependent", that is, not
the response variable.

To complicate things a little further, the requirement they must have if the
glm is to have estimable coefficients, the property they must have is that
they give rise to *linearly* "independent" vectors (in the sense of linear
algebra, another over-use of that word "independent" again) in the data.
There is no requirement that they be mathematically independent, nor is
there any requirement that they be probabilistically independent, (as they
need not be random in any sense).  

The "correlation" you refer to between, say, x and x^2, is true and
relevant, provided you interpret it loosely as a measure of departure from
orthogonality in the geometric sense and not as the measure of linear
association in the probabilistic sense.  If this correlation, when squared,
is too close to 1 it can lead to a numerical failure of linear independence.

When I taught I used to avoid this nomenclature altogether and refer to
"response" and "explanatory" variables.  I still think this would be a
better way of doing things, but linguistic habits are hard to break.  Even
the term "regression" for linear models is a very silly one but here to
stay, I suspect.  I really object, though, when people slip in the adjective
"General" in front of "linear model" like SAS lingo does.  In reality these
are simply Linear Models.  "Multi-"colinearity is another nonsense:
"colinearity" is general enough for all purposes, but "(near) failure of
linear independence" is a clearer way of saying what is going on.

Bill Venables.

