Jacob,

You might consider using VI, which was originally developed as the Unix
standard editor, but it is now available on all platforms. I particularly
like the enhanced VI (called "vim") created by Bram Moolenar
(www.vim.org).

VI is one of those love-or-hate things. It's different. But once you get
the hang of it, you'll find it is very efficient and powerful. 

I like it because I switch between WIN32 and Unix frequently and now can
use the same editor everywhere.

It has syntax coloring for programming languages and it comes with syntax
files for virtually every programming language. But I do not see one for
S or R. But you can create your own or perhaps you can find one somewhere
and customize it.

I'm new to S-Plus (actually R) so I can't say I've used it there much. I
use it with MATLAB and Octave and find it very useful for editing and
replicating data also.

