From phillip.stafford at motorola.com  Wed Oct 31 23:22:59 2001
From: phillip.stafford at motorola.com (Stafford Phillip-APS095)
Date: Wed, 31 Oct 2001 17:22:59 -0600
Subject: Summary multiple density plot
Message-ID: <07077F6C2A38D511920800D0B7821B3201E38821@il06exm01.corp.mot.com>

It seems plotting several density curves in the same graph is a popular thing 
to do so I've summarized some excellent answers:

My old way was to create a dataframe with the data, then select multiple 
columns, and use the GUI density function.  

Thanks to Bert and Naomi, better ways are:

Naomi Robbins from NBR:
I used lines and dots instead of different colors, but the changes should be 
simple.

modpts<-density(mod$staytime, from=0, to=80)
plot(density(ffs$staytime, from=0, to=80))
lines(modpts, type="l")

where mod$staytime and ffs$staytime are the two datasets to be plotted.

changed to:
modpts<-density(mod$staytime, from=0, to=80, col=1)
plot(density(ffs$staytime, from=0, to=80), col=2)
lines(modpts, type="l")

and it did use different colors for the two sets.  Now you just have to decide 
between points and lines.  The code above gives examples of both.

Bert Gunter from Merck:

d1<-density(x1)
d2<-density(x2) ## with whatever density parameters you care to add
plot(d1,col=1,type='l')
lines(d2,col=2)

is a cheap and easy way to do a cheap and easy plot. rug() could be used to
add the data on the horizontal axis.  densityplot() can also be used with 
groups and key parameters in the call to get a fancier plot labelled with a 
legend  (this can also be done above with the older legend() function).

